{
 "id": "z06",
 "text_original": "I needed the SEC's company filings inside a query. I wrote one statement, a REST table with the SEC's required header, and a real filing came back as a row in about five seconds. The second read took under two. I had written no connector. We estimate that saved an hour or more. Not everything was that easy. USAspending's API returned valid JSON, and Zetaris read it as one corrupt record, because the server sends a browser page unless you ask for application JSON. One header fixed it, but we lost time there. Then the moment it clicked. Grok wrote a test bank change into our live payment database, and I re-ran one data-quality rule. The invalid rows went from three to four, and it named the new payment item. No re-registration, no refresh, no pipeline. When the row was deleted, the count went back to three. Zetaris was reading Postgres in place. That rule is now the sweep Probity runs on every check.",
 "text_spoken": "I needed the SEC's company filings inside a query. I wrote one statement, a REST table with the SEC's required header, and a real filing came back as a row in about five seconds. The second read took under two. I had written no connector. We estimate that saved an hour or more. Not everything was that easy. U.S.A. spending's API returned valid JSON, and Zuh-tarris read it as one corrupt record, because the server sends a browser page unless you ask for application JSON. One header fixed it, but we lost time there. Then the moment it clicked. Grok wrote a test bank change into our live payment database, and I re-ran one data-quality rule. The invalid rows went from three to four, and it named the new payment item. No re-registration, no refresh, no pipeline. When the row was deleted, the count went back to three. Zuh-tarris was reading Postgres in place. That rule is now the sweep Probity runs on every check.",
